# Line endings
* text=auto eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Parquet: binary, and never mergeable
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These accumulate across runs and are the durable record, so they must be
# committed (see .gitignore). But git cannot merge binary content, and both
# a local `pixi run transform` and the hourly cron write them — so any
# divergent history produces a conflict git has no way to resolve.
#
# Marking them binary with `-merge` makes git stop *trying*. It still
# reports the conflict, but it won't corrupt the file attempting a
# content merge, and the resolution is unambiguous: take either side, then
# run `pixi run transform`, which rebuilds from data/raw/ and dedupes on
# the source's own record ids. Nothing is lost either way.
#
# Resolution, for whichever file git names:
#     git checkout --theirs data/processed/<file>.parquet
#     git add data/processed/<file>.parquet
#     git rebase --continue
#     pixi run transform      # restores any rows the chosen side lacked
*.parquet binary -merge -diff

# Raw snapshots are JSON with unique filenames per run, so they append
# rather than conflict. Marked -diff only to keep `git log -p` readable.
data/raw/**/*.json -diff
